hostdisk.device for booting AROS from an image file on Hosted MacAROS.

- hostdisk=PATTERN on the kernel command line replaces the host path of
  unit N (/dev/disk%ld); the pattern must hold one %ld and no other
  conversion. Without it the device probes the Mac's own drives.
- Darwin on Apple Silicon: struct stat in place of the 32-bit inode
  request, which the SDK refuses, and the two DKIOC requests of
  <sys/disk.h> spelled out, since that header cannot be included.
- CMD_UPDATE is a barrier. The stock device answered it inside BeginIO,
  so it overtook the writes already queued on the unit port and nothing
  ever reached the host file on purpose. It is now forwarded to the unit
  thread like CMD_WRITE, which replies to it only after the writes ahead
  of it, and the unit thread syncs the host file: fcntl(fd, F_FULLFSYNC)
  on Darwin, spelled out as request 51 because <sys/fcntl.h> cannot be
  included either, falling back to fsync() when the file system does not
  implement it, and fsync() on every other host. "fsync" and "fcntl" are
  added to libcSymbols[] and to struct HostInterface, in the same order.
  A failed sync returns the host errno through error(), so io_Error is
  TDERR_NotSpecified rather than a silent success.

Local to this machine's AROS tree (jonx/AROS, aarch64-darwin-graft); it
is not pushed anywhere. Apply from the AROS source root with
git apply, then build workbench-devs-hostdisk.

